---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets"
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/case-study.astro"
import { getProject } from "@/data/projects"
import envelopeImg from "@/assets/projects/ncga-stationery/envelope-editor.png"

const project = getProject("ncga-variable-data-stationery")!

const pictureFormats: ("avif" | "webp")[] = ["avif", "webp"]
const pictureWidths = [640, 960, 1280]
const pictureSizes = "(min-width: 768px) 960px, 100vw"
---

<CaseStudy
  project={project}
  title="NCGA Variable Data Stationery"
  seoDescription="Variable data stationery for all 170 NC legislators: locked letterhead and envelope templates, a mail-merge pipeline that prints stationery, address, and letter body in one pass, and automated production QA."
>
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    The North Carolina General Assembly print shop produces official stationery
    for all 170 legislators. Every order follows the same formal brand
    guidelines, but each member needs their own contact details, committee
    assignments, and chamber wording. Repeat orders used to rely on a separate
    file for each member, so small differences in spacing, ink color, and
    committee data crept in across all 170.
  </p>
  <p>
    I split the job into two parts: locked templates that keep the stationery
    consistent, and a merge step that adds each recipient’s address and letter
    so the whole mailing prints in one pass.
  </p>

  <div class="template-flow" aria-label="Stationery pipeline">
    <div class="template-flow__node">
      <span>1. Stationery</span>
      <strong>Legislator record into a locked template</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="template-flow__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="template-flow__node">
      <span>2. Merge</span>
      <strong>Recipient address and letter body, one per page</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="template-flow__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="template-flow__node">
      <span>Output</span>
      <strong>One flat PDF, printed in one pass</strong>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Locked Templates</h2>
  <p>
    Each legislator is one record. The template decides what can change and what
    can’t.
  </p>
  <div class="field-lists">
    <div>
      <h3>Locked</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>State seal and official masthead</li>
        <li>Licensed typefaces</li>
        <li>Page grid and margins</li>
        <li>Recycling mark</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>From the record</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Member name and chamber</li>
        <li>Office address and phone</li>
        <li>Committee list</li>
        <li>Ink color</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Visibility toggles hide a section without deleting its data. The browser
    preview renders in exact inches and points, and the PDF renderer shares the
    same logic for chamber copy, ink colors, and visible committees, so the
    preview matches what prints.
  </p>

  <h3>Envelopes</h3>
  <p>
    Envelopes use the same record with a simpler form. There’s no phone number,
    committee list, or state seal, matching the production artwork, and the
    return address is picked from the official legislative buildings instead of
    typed in.
  </p>

  <figure class="case-figure">
    <Picture
      src={envelopeImg}
      alt="Envelope editor with a building picker and a print-accurate envelope preview."
      formats={pictureFormats}
      widths={pictureWidths}
      sizes={pictureSizes}
    />
    <figcaption>
      The envelope editor: a building picker instead of a free-text address, and
      no seal. Recipients are added in the merge step.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>One-Pass Mail Merge</h2>
  <p>
    The personalization comes from a Word mail merge: one letter, or one
    recipient address, per page. The merge step lays the letterhead or envelope
    template onto every page of that output, so the stationery, address, and
    letter print together on the shop’s Xante En/Press digital press.
  </p>
  <p>I built two versions and use whichever the job needs:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Browser merge</strong>, built into the stationery tool. It runs
      entirely in the browser, so files never leave the machine.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Python pipeline</strong> with a terminal UI, for jobs that need
      deeper checks.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Production QA</h2>
  <p>
    The press queue spools slowly when a PDF carries transparency, so both
    versions output flat PDF 1.3 files. Before anything goes to press:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The browser merge blocks the run on a page-size mismatch, which would
      otherwise stretch the letterhead instead of failing.
    </li>
    <li>
      The Python pipeline flattens the letterhead once at the source instead of
      across the whole merged file, then runs a deep transparency audit,
      confirms the page count matches the merge, and checks page dimensions and
      file size.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each finished job is archived with a manifest of its run options and
      verification results.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    Personalized mailings to all 170 members now run in one press pass instead
    of two. It’s the same model as a personalized email send: a locked template,
    one data record per recipient, and QA before anything goes out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Related NCGA work includes the{" "}
    <a href="/projects/print-split-calculator">Print Split Calculator</a>, plus
    identity systems, job tickets, proofing tools, and high-volume production
    workflows.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .template-flow {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
  }

  .template-flow__node {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
    padding: 1rem;
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--background) 96%, var(--muted));
  }

  .template-flow__node span {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .template-flow__node strong {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .template-flow__connector {
    width: 1px;
    height: 1.25rem;
    margin-left: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--border);
  }

  .field-lists {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem 2rem;
  }

  .field-lists h3 {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .field-lists {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .template-flow {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 2rem minmax(0, 1.2fr) 2rem minmax(
          0,
          1fr
        );
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0;
    }

    .template-flow__connector {
      width: 100%;
      height: 1px;
      margin-left: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
